WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:04.910
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.310
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:07.890
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.150
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.290
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.810
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

7
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.990
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

8
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.030
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

9
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.730
Oh, hey there.

10
00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:32.850
Are you ready to break down The Office webisodes, The Accountants?

11
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.030
I'm so ready.

12
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.810
I loved re-watching these.

13
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.010
It was so fun.

14
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.490
I texted Brian Baumgartner this morning.

15
00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:43.590
And I said, Brian, watching these webisodes is making me miss you so much, buddy.

16
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.350
We just had such a good time.

17
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.710
And of course, Oscar, too.

18
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.950
And the three of us have got to get together.

19
00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.690
We have our accountant's dinner, you know.

20
00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:52.350
Yeah.

21
00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.330
So we've got to get it back on the books.

22
00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.950
Well, these were written by Paul Lieberstein and Mike Schur.

23
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.550
And they were directed by Randall Einhorn.

24
00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.470
This was his directorial debut.

25
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.730
Yes, it was so exciting.

26
00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:08.750
I'm going to give you a summary.

27
00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:09.510
Do it.

28
00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.890
The accountants discover that there is $3,000 missing from the company money.

29
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.970
Everyone is a suspect as they interrogate their co-workers to find the missing funds.

30
00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.250
Everyone is on Angela's liSt.

31
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.190
Well, not everyone.

32
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.730
Well, not Roy or Dwight.

33
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.030
Dwight.

34
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:29.770
There's two.

35
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:30.530
Two missing.

36
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:32.970
Guess what?

37
00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.710
I'm going to give you three fast facts today.

38
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.890
It's a rewatch breakdown.

39
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.010
Excuse me.

40
00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.490
I can't get rid of them.

41
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:40.750
Excuse me.

42
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.490
I'm trying so hard.

43
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.450
They will not die.

44
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.190
Just lean into the s*** you love, lady.

45
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:49.670
All right.

46
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.370
Fast fact number one.

47
00:01:52.270 --> 00:02:02.610
The Accountants' webisodes were originally released on July 13, 2016, between Seasons 2 and Seasons 3 on NBC.com.

48
00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.930
This was their way of keeping office viewers engaged over the summer hiatus.

49
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.370
They were 10 mini-episodes.

50
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.370
Each one is about two or three minutes in length.

51
00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:21.910
And on July 13, they released the first two on the same day, but then they spaced them out.

52
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:22.850
You had to wait.

53
00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:23.430
Oh, yeah.

54
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.730
To see the next ones.

55
00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:31.390
You can currently see them on YouTube and also as a special feature on the Season 2 DVD.

56
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.930
Yes, it's disc for everybody.

57
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.310
And they're on Peacock as well.

58
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:40.070
Well, The Accountants' was the first series of webisodes.

59
00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.090
We end up doing more of these in the future, but these were the firSt.

60
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.610
They were groundbreaking.

61
00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.910
I'm going to get into that a little more in Fast Fact number three.

62
00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.270
But, Angela, we had some fan questions for you.

63
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:53.730
Okay.

64
00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.170
The first one is from Amanda G.

65
00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.950
in Northern Ireland who said,

66
00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.950
How did these episodes come about?

67
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.690
And we also heard from Bria D.

68
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.830
in Philly who said,

69
00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:09.010
What was your reaction to finding out you guys were going to do these webisodes?

70
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.210
I remember they reached out to us, and I don't know if they reached out to everyone,

71
00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.810
but I know they reached out to me and Brian and Oscar,

72
00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.810
and they were talking about doing these little things for NBC.com.

73
00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.850
So I remember talking to the folks in the digital department,

74
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.650
and they were saying, you know, we think this would be really fun to do.

75
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.170
And I was like, sure, great.

76
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:36.870
And I was also very excited to have a bigger storyline with the accountants.

77
00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:37.310
Yeah.

78
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.630
You know, we had our running joke, right, that we would do all these bits in the corner.

79
00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.130
They would never make it in.

80
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.510
And I know I've talked about this, that we sort of joked that we had a spinoff,

81
00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.890
but it was on Telemundo, and Oscar said it was called Los Acantadores.

82
00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.890
Angela, Oscar, eat a bean.

83
00:03:53.890 --> 00:04:02.870
I mean, so now here we finally get to have a whole episode, sort of, right, about the accountants.

84
00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.730
Okay, are you ready for fast fact number two?

85
00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.730
I am.

86
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:09.510
This is a fan question from Sarah M.

87
00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.530
in Pennsylvania, who said,

88
00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.930
were the webisodes filmed on the same day as other episodes,

89
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.010
or did the cast come in specifically to film them?

90
00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.490
Well, Sarah, guess who I reached out to?

91
00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.570
The last from the past, Kentopedia.

92
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.410
Kentopedia, and then you also reached out to Angelapedia.

93
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:30.550
I did.

94
00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.130
Let me tell you what I found out, because Kent explained a little bit about how these were produced

95
00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.370
from his point of view, and I'm sure you can confirm a lot of these details, Angela.

96
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.470
He said, y'all came in on a hiatus week.

97
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:44.950
Yeah.

98
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.570
It was in March.

99
00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:53.890
It took two days to film all 10 webisodes, and that they were filmed on March 2nd and March 3rd.

100
00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:54.730
Yes.

101
00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.250
And those were our days off.

102
00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.350
So when we came to set that day, there was no one there.

103
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.750
It was kind of wild.

104
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:05.670
It was like when you go, like, to school, like, after school extracurriculars,

105
00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.250
and there's, like, no one in the building.

106
00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:08.510
Oh, yeah.

107
00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:11.250
I saw, like, the crew call sheet.

108
00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.050
Very small crew.

109
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:18.070
In fact, not only was Randall Einhorn the director of this episode,

110
00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:24.230
but he was also the cinematographer and the camera operator, and there was only one camera.

111
00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.010
Is that true?

112
00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:25.810
That's true.

113
00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.410
Randall did all of that, and then we had one sound person.

114
00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:30.590
It was Ben Patrick.

115
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:31.890
I saw that, too.

116
00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:32.210
Yep.

117
00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.610
And they were on stage with us, but that was pretty much it.

118
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.410
Well, here are the actors who were there both days.

119
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:44.490
It was you, Oscar, Brian, Kate, Phyllis, and Leslie.

120
00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:45.130
Mm-hmm.

121
00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:51.430
And I really noticed when I was re-watching these how Randall had to get very creative

122
00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:56.410
in how he shot things to avoid showing how empty the desks were in the bullpen

123
00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.130
because the whole cast was not there.

124
00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.530
Yes, you're going to see a point of view from the camera that you do not see on the show

125
00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:03.810
that airs every week.

126
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.610
And I loved seeing it.

127
00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.310
I loved seeing this different perspective of the office.

128
00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:13.890
I felt like there were so many angles that we did not normally see on the office.

129
00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:14.290
Yeah.

130
00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:21.410
I got very interested in tracking how Randall was able to shoot things without seeing through

131
00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:22.470
certain windows.

132
00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:22.950
Yes.

133
00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.410
Or into the bullpen.

134
00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:31.610
And also, I was tracking how exactly he used Leslie, Kate, and Phyllis in the background

135
00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:32.310
of things.

136
00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:32.630
Mm-hmm.

137
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:33.490
Strategically.

138
00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:34.050
Yes.

139
00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:38.910
Well, I texted Randall and I said, Randall, I don't know if I ever thanked you for what

140
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:44.090
a wonderful gift it was getting to do these webisodes because it was such a big deal to

141
00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:44.410
us.

142
00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:51.190
And he made it so fun and it looks fantastic and I really think they hold up.

143
00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.950
Well, we got this fan question for you, Angela, and it's kind of about the process of filming.

144
00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.330
It's from Ryan T.

145
00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:03.030
in Aston, Pennsylvania, who said, Angela, what was your favorite part

146
00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.190
of filming this series and how was it different from filming the actual show?

147
00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.230
I guess maybe besides the very small crew, was there anything else?

148
00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:15.890
I just loved getting to have a whole day with Oscar and Brian.

149
00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:23.490
We had so many silly bits that we had made up already those few seasons together, like

150
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.470
our little accounting dynamics that we would play out that rarely made it to the light

151
00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:28.950
of day.

152
00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.510
But we were doing them in the background, you know?

153
00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:36.890
And just to have a day where we showed up and we got to just play all day together,

154
00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:38.590
it was the beSt.

155
00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.830
And I feel like when I rewatched it, I was remembering so many things I'm excited to

156
00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:44.650
share with you guys.

157
00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.170
All right.

158
00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:47.970
Are you ready for Fast Fact No.

159
00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:48.310
3?

160
00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:49.230
I am.

161
00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:50.230
Fast Fact No.

162
00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:54.410
3 is called Very Successful.

163
00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:55.590
It was.

164
00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:03.850
These webisodes received millions of views on NBC.com.

165
00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:06.370
They broke the mold.

166
00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:14.570
They invented, in many ways, this idea of supplemental content for streaming or for websites.

167
00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:15.330
Yes.

168
00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:19.510
You know, I had been emailing Joya Balfour, who helped us so much.

169
00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:20.970
She worked for NBC Digital.

170
00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:27.070
And she said the accountants was really when NBC saw that digital content for the office

171
00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:28.650
had a ton of potential.

172
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.530
It's not just NBC that realized it.

173
00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:38.090
Kent said that after the success of the webisodes, he was contacted by almost every production

174
00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:42.010
executive from every studio asking how we did it.

175
00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:42.670
Wow.

176
00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:47.550
But not only that, the webisodes won two awards.

177
00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:54.790
They won a Webby Award for Video Comedy Short Form and an Emmy for Outstanding Broadband Program

178
00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:55.590
Comedy.

179
00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.950
Angela, what was that experience like?

180
00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:00.410
You won an Emmy.

181
00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:01.450
I know.

182
00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:07.770
Well, I thought to really have that experience, we should have the Emmy here with us today.

183
00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:08.330
What?

184
00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.470
You brought it?

185
00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.170
Oh, my gosh.

186
00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.650
I see this in your house all the time.

187
00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.630
And I, oh, my gosh.

188
00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.230
I love it, lady.

189
00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.330
It's very large.

190
00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:22.610
Wow.

191
00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.630
That is so awesome.

192
00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:26.430
There you go.

193
00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:27.830
Can I see it?

194
00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:28.630
I want to hold it.

195
00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:29.870
Watch out.

196
00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.330
The wings are really pointy.

197
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:33.910
They're very pointy.

198
00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:41.070
Well, the only time I got to hold an Emmy was when our show won an Emmy in season two.

199
00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:42.570
I did not get to keep it.

200
00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.150
I got to hold it for the pictures and then give it back.

201
00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.650
And it has your name on it.

202
00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.230
I know.

203
00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:51.130
Mm-hmm.

204
00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.950
That is f***ing rad.

205
00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.950
Well, I thought I would read to you what it says on my Emmy.

206
00:09:58.330 --> 00:09:58.830
Yes.

207
00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:07.210
It says, 2006 to 2007 Daytime Entertainment Emmy Awards, Outstanding Broadband Program Comedy,

208
00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:12.690
The Office, Accountants, NBC, Angela Kinsey, Performer.

209
00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.270
So freaking cool.

210
00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:16.130
Oh, it's heavy.

211
00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.410
So who all was at the ceremony?

212
00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:19.710
Who'd you get to celebrate with?

213
00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:20.650
Was Randall there?

214
00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:22.190
What was that night like?

215
00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:26.510
Well, first of all, we couldn't believe we had been nominated for an Emmy for the webisodes.

216
00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.630
We didn't even know there was a category for that.

217
00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:35.850
I mean, we really were so just surprised and excited just because they were nominated.

218
00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:42.970
And I remember we went, a big group of us went with the NBC digital team, and none of

219
00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:44.390
us were prepared to give a speech.

220
00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:45.890
None of us wrote anything.

221
00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:47.570
We didn't think we would win.

222
00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.950
We just were so excited to go.

223
00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:53.430
And lady, I do have a journal entry.

224
00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:56.610
I wrote in my journal about my outfit for the night.

225
00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:57.710
Would you like to hear it?

226
00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.110
Of course I would.

227
00:10:59.310 --> 00:10:59.950
Yes.

228
00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:01.170
I wrote in my journal.

229
00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:02.910
Sam, you're going to love this.

230
00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:03.210
Ready?

231
00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:05.390
Okay.

232
00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.390
I found a dress for the Daytime Emmys, exclamation point.

233
00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:10.470
Woo-hoo!

234
00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:11.970
Exclamation point.

235
00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.090
And it doesn't need to be altered.

236
00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:15.530
Double woo-hoo.

237
00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.550
I got it at Arden B in the mall.

238
00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.030
It's a black and white beaded mini dress.

239
00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:23.570
I don't have shoes yet.

240
00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:24.770
Wish me luck with that.

241
00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:26.670
Who's it to?

242
00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:27.250
Who am I?

243
00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:27.850
Who am I?

244
00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:29.010
Who's wishing me luck, you guys?

245
00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.210
Is this a letter home?

246
00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:32.130
Or it's just your journal?

247
00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:33.650
It's just my journal about it.

248
00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:33.950
Okay.

249
00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:34.730
And then I had a bunch.

250
00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.170
I have a bunch of pictures.

251
00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:37.770
So I have a bunch of stuff.

252
00:11:37.890 --> 00:11:38.010
Okay.

253
00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:43.250
And then I said, also, I wore a black and white dress to the other Emmys last year.

254
00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.630
What's up with me in black and white dresses?

255
00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:46.830
You're doing bits?

256
00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.010
Double question mark.

257
00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.450
Double question mark.

258
00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.050
We'll fill you in after.

259
00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.530
And then I never wrote again.

260
00:11:57.830 --> 00:11:58.230
After.

261
00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.930
You never let yourself know how it went.

262
00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:01.870
We'll fill you in.

263
00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.030
Did you get shoes?

264
00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:03.590
I don't know.

265
00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:04.150
We don't know.

266
00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.030
I don't know.

267
00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.730
I don't know if I got shoes.

268
00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.970
But my dress was from Arden B.

269
00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:09.630
in the mall.

270
00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:13.070
So when we won, we really were.

271
00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:13.910
It's like that moment.

272
00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:15.650
It sounds like a cliche where you're like, what?

273
00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.670
I can't believe it.

274
00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.670
And the three of us went up, Brian, Oscar, and me.

275
00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:24.310
And I think we sort of stumbled through an acceptance speech.

276
00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:27.030
And then we went to this party afterwards.

277
00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:28.730
There was like a rooftop patio.

278
00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:31.030
And we took a bunch of press pictures.

279
00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:35.990
Jenna, in all of the pictures, even with the big group, with the big team of NBC Digital,

280
00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.550
I'm holding the Emmy.

281
00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.210
I'm always the one holding the Emmy.

282
00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:41.190
I'm like, did I not let anyone else hold it?

283
00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:44.090
And at one, I lift it high over my head.

284
00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:49.410
And I thought, oh, no, please don't take anyone out like I did with Phyllis and Steve's Golden Globe.

285
00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:50.430
That's your favorite thing.

286
00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:51.170
I guess.

287
00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:54.050
It's knocking someone with a trophy.

288
00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.430
It was such, such a fun night.

289
00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:02.970
And I just remember it just being such a highlight of my time in the office.

290
00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:09.870
Well, you mentioned that we've been talking with Brian Baumgartner because he's going to be a guest on an upcoming episode.

291
00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:13.230
But we ended up also chatting with him about the webisodes.

292
00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:18.650
And he sent in an audio clip of his memories of doing the webisodes and of that Emmy night.

293
00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:19.410
Let's listen.

294
00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.110
I remember that first webisode series, The Accountants, being so much fun.

295
00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:41.410
Angela and Oscar, of course, and I getting to really play for the first times and almost being stars of our own show there for a few days with the rest of the gang mostly gone.

296
00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:42.170
Mostly gone.

297
00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.150
I remember having such a great time.

298
00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:52.670
And when we eventually won the Emmy for that series, I remember that being one of the most fun nights.

299
00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:59.510
Angela, you and Oscar and I having a party that night when we won the Emmy.

300
00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:10.470
Well, it's one of my favorite and most fondest memories of our outside life while we were filming the show.

301
00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.090
Oh, same.

302
00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:15.990
I have so many fun pictures from that night, too, that I can share.

303
00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:18.750
That's when, Jenna, I got bangs.

304
00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:20.390
Oh, really?

305
00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:20.850
Yeah.

306
00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:22.730
I'm sure that's a journal entry.

307
00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:24.170
Go look for her.

308
00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:26.130
That was a big swing for me.

309
00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:27.050
I got bangs.

310
00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:31.010
And did you guys also go to a ceremony when you won the Webby?

311
00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:35.570
Well, actually, I'm not sure why, but Brian and Oscar weren't able to go.

312
00:14:35.750 --> 00:14:37.450
I can't remember why, but I did.

313
00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:44.350
I went there with Joya from NBC Digital, and I accepted on behalf of the accountants, the Webby.

314
00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:45.930
Did you wear your same dress?

315
00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:46.650
I didn't.

316
00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:47.910
I wore a different dress.

317
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:48.470
I don't know.

318
00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:50.550
I don't have any journal entry about this dress.

319
00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:51.970
It's just a little black dress.

320
00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:57.410
And you know how at the Webby's, you can only say five words for your acceptance speech?

321
00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:57.990
Yes.

322
00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.690
You remember this because we won a Webby in 2022.

323
00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:07.030
The Office Ladies is a Webby's People's Voice winner in podcasts, television, and film.

324
00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.850
Do you remember what our acceptance speech was?

325
00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:15.050
I believe it ended with, that's what she said.

326
00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.950
Yes, it did, and I found it.

327
00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:20.250
But what was the first word?

328
00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:21.010
Here we are.

329
00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:22.590
Speechless.

330
00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:23.930
That's what she said.

331
00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:25.770
There it is.

332
00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.530
Well, I was curious.

333
00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:30.010
I was like, what did I say?

334
00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.130
What were my five words?

335
00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:31.890
I couldn't remember.

336
00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:36.810
And I knew it was like something about accounting, and I reached out to Joya, and she said, Angela,

337
00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:37.350
I remember.

338
00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:38.190
I have it here.

339
00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:39.210
And she sent it to me.

340
00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:43.750
Our acceptance speech was, accounting is such a riot.

341
00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:48.850
And I said it just like that with my snarky Angela Martin face.

342
00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:50.110
Amazing.

343
00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:51.970
So there you go.

344
00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:57.770
Well, something I think we should share is that you all filmed these for free.

345
00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:00.690
Mike and Paul wrote these for free.

346
00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:09.650
And yes, you won two awards, but you have never received any compensation for these webisodes.

347
00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:13.770
And NBC continues to monetize them on YouTube and Peacock.

348
00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:16.250
You do not receive residuals on them.

349
00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:20.230
And that is because this was what we were saying before.

350
00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.290
This was all new territory.

351
00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:26.990
It was originally seen as marketing and publicity for the show.

352
00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:27.630
Yeah.

353
00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:34.270
But after their success, you know, our unions, SAG and WGA,

354
00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:42.450
started to rethink how actors should be compensated for creating original content outside of the normal network broadcaSt.

355
00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:56.950
I mean, the success of the webisodes really brought into focus all the ways that talent could become exploited if we didn't get some guardrails on this new medium.

356
00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:57.870
That's right.

357
00:16:58.050 --> 00:16:58.510
That's right.

358
00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:02.050
I think that's kind of a cool thing that our show did.

359
00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:03.410
Yeah, I do too.

360
00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:12.750
And, you know, and we know people think that we sit around collecting like millions of dollars a year on residuals from the office.

361
00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:14.590
And we don't.

362
00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:20.030
We do not receive residuals on Peacock or Netflix or any of the streamers.

363
00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:21.090
We never have.

364
00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:24.970
And that is because all of our contracts predated streaming.

365
00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:26.990
It literally did not exiSt.

366
00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:32.910
There was no language in our unions or in our contracts for how we would be compensated for it.

367
00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:36.830
Digital content was so new when we started the show.

368
00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:38.830
I mean, clearly it was brand new.

369
00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:39.490
Yes.

370
00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:46.190
Anyway, I would like to thank the webisodes for kicking off that conversation for Artists Everywhere.

371
00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.210
Oh, I love that.

372
00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:56.230
Well, Jenna, just to wrap this up really quickly before we get into the breakdown, if you'll notice, I just brought in my Emmy.

373
00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:57.690
I didn't bring in the Webby.

374
00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:58.250
Yeah.

375
00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.010
Because the Webby, I think, went to NBC Digital.

376
00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:03.250
Like, I didn't leave New York with a Webby.

377
00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:03.750
Okay.

378
00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:10.470
But when we won this Daytime Emmy, they handed us this one Emmy to hold as we did our –

379
00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:11.410
The photo op Emmy.

380
00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:12.350
The photo op Emmy.

381
00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.950
And I just said, I'm going to take it home.

382
00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:16.270
And they were like, what?

383
00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:19.310
And I was like, yeah, I'm going to take it home, and then you should send Brian an Oscar one, too.

384
00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:26.450
And there was this look – there was this moment where they were like, that's not really your – and I was like, thank you.

385
00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:26.830
Bye.

386
00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.170
Lady, I love that.

387
00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:30.930
I love that you did that.

388
00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:34.230
You were like, I am not going to walk up on an Emmy stage again.

389
00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.810
Because we didn't get one when we won the show.

390
00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:38.510
No, none of the performers got it.

391
00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:39.490
It went to the producers.

392
00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:40.270
Yeah.

393
00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:41.990
So I went home with the trophy.

394
00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:43.850
It had no writing on it.

395
00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:48.050
And a few weeks later, in the mail, I got the plaque that went around it.

396
00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:51.330
I don't think I was meant to get this to take home.

397
00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.050
I think it's supposed to be on a shelf somewhere at NBC.

398
00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.610
Well, it's not.

399
00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.790
It's on our podcasting table right now.

400
00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:59.690
And Brian and Oscar got one, too.

401
00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:00.450
Well done.

402
00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:02.650
Well, those are our fast facts.

403
00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:03.550
Why don't we take a break?

